meta {
  name: rate-service-wrong-vocabulary
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

put {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/guest/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/service-review
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Guest-Token: {{guest_token}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "rating": 4,
    "tags": ["tasty"]
  }
}

tests {
  test("refuses a dish tag on a service rating", function () {
    // A DELIBERATE REFUSAL. `tasty` is a perfectly valid tag -- on a dish. The two vocabularies
    // are separate types in Go (models.ReviewTag and models.ServiceTag) precisely so one cannot
    // be used where the other belongs.
    expect(res.status).to.equal(422)
  })

  test("with the SERVICE vocabulary's own code", function () {
    // Not TX_REV_003, which is the dish vocabulary's refusal. Distinct codes so a client is told
    // which of the two closed sets it got wrong rather than left to guess.
    expect(res.body.code).to.equal("TX_REV_009")
  })

  test("and the earlier rating is untouched", function () {
    expect(res.body.data).to.be.undefined
  })
}

docs {
  # A dish tag on a service rating

The dish and service vocabularies are separate closed sets, and crossing them is a 422 rather than
a silent drop -- the value of a fixed vocabulary is that every stored tag can be counted.

Both live in one place each: `backend/internal/models/review.go` (`ReviewTag`, `ServiceTag`) and
`packages/shared/src/review.ts`.
}
